Subject: Draw an overlay window of a forced app at the density of the monitor it is on

DSA shows each app overlay window (TYPE_APPLICATION_OVERLAY and the older system alert types) in
a host window of its own, on whatever monitor the user drags it to. An app set to forced system
scaling draws at the density of the monitor its task's window is on (0009, 0010), and its process
draws its overlays at that density too, wherever they are. On a monitor of another density the
host then has to stretch the overlay: FLO's floating lyrics, drawn at 164 dpi for a 125% monitor,
were shown 1.6 times larger on a 200% one, the right size but blurred. An overlay is in no task,
so the density 0009 gives a task never reaches it.

The window manager's shell command "dsa-overlay-density <WINDOW> <DPI>" now gives one overlay
window a density of its own, and "dsa-overlay-density <WINDOW> 0" takes it away. WINDOW is the
identity hash that starts the window's name, as SurfaceFlinger's list of windows (which the DSA
agent reads) names it. Only a window of an overlay type that an app added is taken. The window
manager keeps the density on the window (WindowState.mDsaDensityDpi, which the command prints
when given no DPI) and tells the window's process with IWindow.executeCommand
(DsaDensityRescale.WINDOW_DENSITY_COMMAND), as "dump-visible-window-views" reaches a window's
views. Without arguments the command prints "supported", which is how the agent tells an image
with this change from one without it. The DSA agent, which holds MANAGE_ACTIVITY_TASKS, gives
the overlay of an app set to forced system scaling the density of its monitor when that differs
from its task's, and takes it away when the overlay is back on a monitor of its task's density.

Android has no per-window density for such a window. The window manager does send each window a
merged configuration, but a window that is not an activity's and was not added through a window
context (an overlay a service adds with its own context, as FLO's is) takes its density from its
context's resources, which belong to the whole process: ViewRootImpl.performConfigurationChange
calls updateConfiguration, which reads mView.getResources(). Changing those would change every
window of the app. So the window's own views are converted instead, in the app's process, with
0010's conversion:

- ViewRootImpl.mDsaDensityDpi holds the window's density. View.dsaTargetDensity is that density
  for a view in the window, and the view's resources' density everywhere else, and 0010's
  dsaRescaleIfStale converts a view to it. ViewRootImpl.dsaSetWindowDensity converts the
  window's views in place (DsaDensityRescale.rescaleWindow) and lays the window out again as
  0010 does for an activity's windows. A view that joins the window later is converted when it
  is attached (View.dispatchAttachedToWindow), one that leaves it is converted back the first
  time it is touched.
- The window's size: an app gives an overlay's width and height in pixels at its density
  (WindowManager.LayoutParams), so the window takes the size the app last gave it at the
  window's density, also when the app updates its layout parameters (as it does to move it).
  Its position stays as the app gives it: the app moves it by the distances the pointer went.
- What the app sets in the window afterwards: a text size in dp or sp
  (TextView.setTextSize), and an image or background from its resources (ImageView,
  View.setBackgroundDrawable, View.setForeground) are taken at the window's density.

Not converted, as in 0010: sizes the app works out itself from its resources or display metrics
and sets in pixels. They stay at the app's density in that window.

Nothing changes in a process that no overlay window was given a density: the host gives one only
to an overlay of an app set to forced system scaling, a view checks nothing until then (every new
check in a view stands behind DsaDensityRescale.sActive, as 0010's do; a window only notes the size
its app gives it), and no other window of any app is touched.

Applies to frameworks/base at 45034f0663f960d9ee5fb0a101a4732b71f6e2f4 (Android 16), after 0011.
